{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-167932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of a circle with radius $r$  centered at the point $(a,b)$ in rectangular coordinates in standard form is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the circle is centered at $(3,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=3$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the circle in rectangular coordinates is \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167932" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c501bc99dfe9b7a72ad6c34b5b006de6d5529b47a53db676821ebec18bd518"} {"question" "q-167932" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167932" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9-(x-3)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^{2}} \\\\ |y| & =\\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}-6x+9)} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n* the equation with the positive square root, $y=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$, describes the upper semicircle, and \n\n* the equation with the negative square root, $y=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$, describes the lower semicircle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c501bc99dfe9b7a72ad6c34b5b006de6d5529b47a53db676821ebec18bd518"} {"question" "q-167933" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167933" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167933" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167933" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since  $y=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the upper semicircle  and  $y=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the lower semicircle, the area $A$ of the circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}[(\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}})-(-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}2\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167934" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c501bc99dfe9b7a72ad6c34b5b006de6d5529b47a53db676821ebec18bd518"} {"question" "q-167934" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167934" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${f}_{1}(x)=\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the upper semicircle and ${f}_{2}(x)=-\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}$ represents the lower semicircle with $a=0$ and $b=6.$ \n\nFirst, we find the derivative of ${f}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3-x}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}_{1}^{′}(x)$ into the arc length formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}_{1} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}_{1}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(3-x)^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\sqrt{\\frac{6x-{x}^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9-6x+{x}^{2}}{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the same manner, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L={L}_{1}+{L}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167935" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c501bc99dfe9b7a72ad6c34b5b006de6d5529b47a53db676821ebec18bd518"} {"question" "q-167935" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167935" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In general, the area of the region bounded by a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ and the rays $\\theta ={\\theta }_{1}$ and $\\theta ={\\theta }_{2}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of our circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}36{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}18{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c501bc99dfe9b7a72ad6c34b5b006de6d5529b47a53db676821ebec18bd518"} {"question" "q-167937" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167937" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18(\\cos(\\theta))^2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nGiven that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $18(\\cos(\\theta))^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConstruct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "32c501bc99dfe9b7a72ad6c34b5b006de6d5529b47a53db676821ebec18bd518"} {"question" "q-167938" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167938" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/assets/ae44c9f19c7afc26c399999105b7d17c6e2e6b3e020e7b419065a8514741dc9b.png)\n\n \n\nConsider a circle with radius $3$ centered at the point $(3,0),$ shown above.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWrite the equation of this circle in the form $(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $(x-3)^2+(y-0)^2=9$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nWrite the equations of the upper and lower semicircles.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Upper: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Lower: $\\,\\,\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the area of this circle using rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2\\sqrt{9-(x-3)^2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nConstruct an integral that represents the arc length of this circle in rectangular coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{0}^{6}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6x-x^2}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nGiven that the polar equation of the circle is $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ construct an integral that represents the area of this circle in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $18(\\cos(\\theta))^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nConstruct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe average distance from the origin to a point on the circumference of the circle is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $r(\\theta )$ represents the distance of each point on the circle from the origin, the average distance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6-(-6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067768/state.json" "file_sha256" "685539746eef814611852bedd896ea5cb405a1cfa82a8095c7ffe795d62bb5e2"} {"question" "q-167939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/